## The idea

Tasks and their subtasks form a **dependency graph**. A task can run when every subtask has **completed**. So for each task keep a count of unfinished dependencies; when it reaches 0 the task goes into a "ready" queue. Completing a task lowers the count of every task waiting on it. Before accepting new tasks, check the **combined** graph (old + new edges) for a cycle with a depth-first search; if there is one, throw and change nothing.

## Step by step

1. AddTask: walk the new task and all its subtasks (with a stack) and collect their ids and dependency lists — without touching the manager yet.
2. Build the merged graph = existing edges + new edges (union, never overwrite).
3. Depth-first search from the new task. A task we meet again while it is still "on the current path" closes a cycle → throw CycleException. Nothing has been changed, so the manager stays as it was.
4. Commit: register new tasks, add each new edge once, and count unfinished dependencies. Tasks with 0 go to the ready queue.
5. PollNextTask: take ready tasks from the queue; skip any that gained a dependency or were already handed out.
6. CompleteTask: mark it done (twice is harmless), and for every task waiting on it decrement the count; at 0 it becomes ready.

## Worked example

**Input:** deploy → build → compile  (deploy needs build, build needs compile)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| AddTask(deploy) | counts: compile 0, build 1, deploy 1 → ready = [compile] |
| Poll | returns compile |
| Poll | nothing ready → null |
| Complete(compile) | build waits on compile → build count 1 → 0 → ready = [build] |
| Poll, Complete(build) | returns build; then deploy count → 0 → ready |
| Poll | returns deploy |

**Result:** compile, null, build, deploy — in dependency order

## Cost

AddTask: O(V + E) for the new part plus the cycle check over the merged graph. PollNextTask and CompleteTask: O(1) amortised per task / edge. Memory O(V + E).

